1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,084
This is Russia.

2
00:00:01,084 --> 00:00:03,118
The largest country on Earth,

3
00:00:03,118 --> 00:00:03,660
by far.

4
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:07,900
It covers more than seventeen million square kilometers.

5
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:10,835
That's more than the entire surface of the

6
00:00:10,835 --> 00:00:12,200
dwarf planet Pluto.

7
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:13,950
And yet Bangladesh,

8
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:17,240
a country more than one hundred times smaller,

9
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:18,430
has more people.

10
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:21,445
Most of this giant is almost empty.

11
00:00:21,445 --> 00:00:22,111
Forests,

12
00:00:22,111 --> 00:00:24,036
frozen ground and rivers,

13
00:00:24,036 --> 00:00:26,183
for thousands of kilometers,

14
00:00:26,183 --> 00:00:28,330
with hardly a town in sight.

15
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:31,178
So how did one country end up with

16
00:00:31,178 --> 00:00:32,177
so much land,

17
00:00:32,177 --> 00:00:32,819
and why,

18
00:00:32,819 --> 00:00:34,389
after four centuries,

19
00:00:34,389 --> 00:00:36,530
is so much of it still empty?

20
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:41,302
By the end of this video,

21
00:00:41,302 --> 00:00:43,053
you'll see how the cold,

22
00:00:43,053 --> 00:00:45,295
rivers that flow the wrong way,

23
00:00:45,295 --> 00:00:47,748
a band of fur hunters and one very

24
00:00:47,748 --> 00:00:50,480
long railway shaped the map of Russia.

25
00:00:50,830 --> 00:00:51,305
First,

26
00:00:51,305 --> 00:00:51,984
the size.

27
00:00:51,984 --> 00:00:54,768
Russia is almost twice as big as Canada,

28
00:00:54,768 --> 00:00:56,669
the second largest country,

29
00:00:56,669 --> 00:00:59,249
and about as big as the United States

30
00:00:59,249 --> 00:01:01,150
and Australia put together.

31
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:02,371
Altogether,

32
00:01:02,371 --> 00:01:05,128
Russia holds more than a tenth of all

33
00:01:05,128 --> 00:01:06,870
the land on our planet.

34
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,815
It is so wide that it stretches across

35
00:01:09,815 --> 00:01:11,079
eleven time zones.

36
00:01:11,079 --> 00:01:13,741
When people in Moscow are going to bed,

37
00:01:13,741 --> 00:01:15,138
people in Kamchatka,

38
00:01:15,138 --> 00:01:16,203
on the Pacific,

39
00:01:16,203 --> 00:01:17,800
are already getting up.

40
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:20,686
From the Baltic Sea in the west to

41
00:01:20,686 --> 00:01:22,932
the Bering Strait in the east,

42
00:01:22,932 --> 00:01:26,482
Russia stretches about nine thousand kilometers.

43
00:01:26,482 --> 00:01:29,670
It touches two continents and three oceans.

44
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:33,245
It shares land borders with fourteen countries,

45
00:01:33,245 --> 00:01:36,269
from Norway and Finland in the north-west to

46
00:01:36,269 --> 00:01:38,890
China and North Korea in the far east.

47
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:40,926
And at its eastern tip,

48
00:01:40,926 --> 00:01:44,580
Russia is only about eighty kilometers from Alaska,

49
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:46,407
across the Bering Strait.

50
00:01:46,407 --> 00:01:47,461
Remember that,

51
00:01:47,461 --> 00:01:49,990
because it becomes important later.

52
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:51,339
So on paper,

53
00:01:51,339 --> 00:01:54,030
Russia has room for almost anyone.

54
00:01:54,030 --> 00:01:55,337
Room for cities,

55
00:01:55,337 --> 00:01:56,182
for farms,

56
00:01:56,182 --> 00:01:58,950
for hundreds of millions of people.

57
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:02,094
But today it has around one hundred and

58
00:02:02,094 --> 00:02:03,491
forty-five million.

59
00:02:03,491 --> 00:02:04,538
Spread evenly,

60
00:02:04,538 --> 00:02:08,170
that's fewer than nine people per square kilometer.

61
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:09,601
For comparison,

62
00:02:09,601 --> 00:02:12,640
Germany has more than two hundred people per

63
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,856
square kilometer,

64
00:02:13,856 --> 00:02:17,300
and even the United States has around thirty-five.

65
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:20,196
But averages hide the real story.

66
00:02:20,196 --> 00:02:21,619
Because in Russia,

67
00:02:21,619 --> 00:02:24,390
people are not spread evenly at all.

68
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:28,533
Look at this line.

69
00:02:28,533 --> 00:02:30,982
The Ural Mountains run about two and a

70
00:02:30,982 --> 00:02:34,436
half thousand kilometers from the Arctic Ocean towards

71
00:02:34,436 --> 00:02:35,190
Kazakhstan.

72
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:37,465
They are old and fairly low.

73
00:02:37,465 --> 00:02:40,586
The highest peak is under two thousand meters.

74
00:02:40,586 --> 00:02:43,773
But for centuries geographers have used them as

75
00:02:43,773 --> 00:02:46,030
the line between Europe and Asia.

76
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:49,293
West of the Urals is European Russia.

77
00:02:49,293 --> 00:02:51,593
East of them is Asian Russia:

78
00:02:51,593 --> 00:02:53,587
Siberia and the Far East.

79
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:56,960
The difference between the two is enormous.

80
00:02:57,310 --> 00:02:59,961
Roughly three out of every four Russians live

81
00:02:59,961 --> 00:03:01,229
in the European part,

82
00:03:01,229 --> 00:03:03,361
which is only about a quarter of the

83
00:03:03,361 --> 00:03:03,880
country.

84
00:03:04,230 --> 00:03:06,425
That's where the big cities are.

85
00:03:06,425 --> 00:03:06,958
Moscow,

86
00:03:06,958 --> 00:03:08,155
Saint Petersburg,

87
00:03:08,155 --> 00:03:09,286
Nizhny Novgorod,

88
00:03:09,286 --> 00:03:09,752
Kazan.

89
00:03:09,752 --> 00:03:12,080
All of them west of the mountains.

90
00:03:12,430 --> 00:03:15,574
Moscow alone has about thirteen million people.

91
00:03:15,574 --> 00:03:18,194
That's more than all of the Russian Far

92
00:03:18,194 --> 00:03:19,438
East put together,

93
00:03:19,438 --> 00:03:21,862
a region more than twice the size of

94
00:03:21,862 --> 00:03:22,320
India.

95
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:24,258
And east of the Urals,

96
00:03:24,258 --> 00:03:27,020
people cluster in a thin line along the

97
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:28,815
southern edge of Siberia,

98
00:03:28,815 --> 00:03:31,439
close to the borders with Kazakhstan,

99
00:03:31,439 --> 00:03:32,820
Mongolia and China.

100
00:03:33,170 --> 00:03:34,492
Yekaterinburg,

101
00:03:34,492 --> 00:03:35,021
Omsk,

102
00:03:35,021 --> 00:03:36,167
Novosibirsk,

103
00:03:36,167 --> 00:03:37,313
Krasnoyarsk,

104
00:03:37,313 --> 00:03:38,106
Irkutsk.

105
00:03:38,106 --> 00:03:41,985
Almost every big Siberian city sits on that

106
00:03:41,985 --> 00:03:43,660
one southern line.

107
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:44,819
North of it,

108
00:03:44,819 --> 00:03:47,682
there are areas larger than France with fewer

109
00:03:47,682 --> 00:03:49,300
people than a small town.

110
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:54,011
Siberia alone covers around thirteen million square kilometers.

111
00:03:54,011 --> 00:03:55,510
If it were a country,

112
00:03:55,510 --> 00:03:58,303
it would still be the largest country on

113
00:03:58,303 --> 00:03:58,780
Earth.

114
00:03:59,130 --> 00:04:01,973
So the real question isn't why Russia has

115
00:04:01,973 --> 00:04:02,989
so few people.

116
00:04:02,989 --> 00:04:05,155
It's why so few of them live in

117
00:04:05,155 --> 00:04:07,660
three quarters of their own country.

118
00:04:10,610 --> 00:04:13,508
The first answer is the one you'd expect.

119
00:04:13,508 --> 00:04:14,198
The cold.

120
00:04:14,198 --> 00:04:16,751
But Siberian cold is on a level most

121
00:04:16,751 --> 00:04:18,200
of us can't imagine.

122
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:20,474
In the village of Oymyakon,

123
00:04:20,474 --> 00:04:21,298
in Yakutia,

124
00:04:21,298 --> 00:04:25,283
a temperature of around minus sixty-eight degrees Celsius

125
00:04:25,283 --> 00:04:26,588
has been recorded.

126
00:04:26,588 --> 00:04:30,573
It's often called the coldest permanently inhabited place

127
00:04:30,573 --> 00:04:31,260
on Earth.

128
00:04:31,610 --> 00:04:33,315
Not far away is Yakutsk,

129
00:04:33,315 --> 00:04:36,248
a city of more than three hundred thousand

130
00:04:36,248 --> 00:04:36,794
people,

131
00:04:36,794 --> 00:04:40,204
where the average temperature in January is below

132
00:04:40,204 --> 00:04:41,500
minus thirty-five.

133
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:44,632
Siberia is so cold because it's so far

134
00:04:44,632 --> 00:04:45,773
from the ocean.

135
00:04:45,773 --> 00:04:47,128
Water stores heat,

136
00:04:47,128 --> 00:04:50,124
and far inland there is nothing to soften

137
00:04:50,124 --> 00:04:50,980
the winter.

138
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:54,710
The warm Atlantic air that keeps Western Europe

139
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:57,808
mild loses its strength as it travels east.

140
00:04:57,808 --> 00:05:00,061
By the time it reaches Siberia,

141
00:05:00,061 --> 00:05:02,103
there's almost nothing left.

142
00:05:02,103 --> 00:05:04,567
And the Arctic is wide open to the

143
00:05:04,567 --> 00:05:05,060
north.

144
00:05:05,410 --> 00:05:07,057
The cold has a second,

145
00:05:07,057 --> 00:05:08,131
hidden effect.

146
00:05:08,131 --> 00:05:11,568
Around two thirds of Russia sits on permafrost,

147
00:05:11,568 --> 00:05:14,504
ground that stays frozen all year round,

148
00:05:14,504 --> 00:05:17,010
sometimes hundreds of meters deep.

149
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:19,433
Building on it is a nightmare.

150
00:05:19,433 --> 00:05:22,577
A heated house slowly melts the ground beneath

151
00:05:22,577 --> 00:05:22,844
it,

152
00:05:22,844 --> 00:05:25,720
and the building starts to tilt and crack.

153
00:05:26,070 --> 00:05:27,833
So in cities like Yakutsk,

154
00:05:27,833 --> 00:05:31,164
buildings stand on concrete piles driven deep into

155
00:05:31,164 --> 00:05:32,405
the frozen ground,

156
00:05:32,405 --> 00:05:34,952
with a gap underneath so that cold air

157
00:05:34,952 --> 00:05:36,650
can flow below the floor.

158
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,826
Then there are the rivers.

159
00:05:38,826 --> 00:05:41,667
Siberia has some of the largest rivers on

160
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:42,141
Earth:

161
00:05:42,141 --> 00:05:42,682
the Ob,

162
00:05:42,682 --> 00:05:44,441
the Yenisey and the Lena.

163
00:05:44,441 --> 00:05:46,470
But look which way they flow.

164
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:47,327
North.

165
00:05:47,327 --> 00:05:48,994
Into the Arctic Ocean,

166
00:05:48,994 --> 00:05:51,747
which is frozen for much of the year.

167
00:05:51,747 --> 00:05:53,631
Not towards any warm sea,

168
00:05:53,631 --> 00:05:55,950
and not towards any big market.

169
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:56,777
Worse,

170
00:05:56,777 --> 00:05:59,845
in spring the southern parts of these rivers

171
00:05:59,845 --> 00:06:00,663
melt first,

172
00:06:00,663 --> 00:06:03,526
while their mouths in the north are still

173
00:06:03,526 --> 00:06:04,071
frozen.

174
00:06:04,071 --> 00:06:06,048
The water has nowhere to go,

175
00:06:06,048 --> 00:06:07,820
and it floods huge areas.

176
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:08,951
In summer,

177
00:06:08,951 --> 00:06:11,577
the thawed surface turns into swamp.

178
00:06:11,577 --> 00:06:15,196
Western Siberia holds some of the largest wetlands

179
00:06:15,196 --> 00:06:16,190
in the world.

180
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:19,123
And over all of it grows the taiga,

181
00:06:19,123 --> 00:06:21,203
the largest forest on Earth.

182
00:06:21,203 --> 00:06:24,073
Russia alone holds about a fifth of all

183
00:06:24,073 --> 00:06:25,580
the world's forests.

184
00:06:25,930 --> 00:06:27,005
Further south,

185
00:06:27,005 --> 00:06:28,438
the soil is better,

186
00:06:28,438 --> 00:06:30,803
but the growing season is short.

187
00:06:30,803 --> 00:06:32,237
In much of Siberia,

188
00:06:32,237 --> 00:06:35,820
frost-free summers last only around three months.

189
00:06:36,170 --> 00:06:37,984
Russia's best farmland,

190
00:06:37,984 --> 00:06:39,797
the famous black earth,

191
00:06:39,797 --> 00:06:41,611
lies in the south-west,

192
00:06:41,611 --> 00:06:43,425
near the European part.

193
00:06:43,425 --> 00:06:44,332
And people,

194
00:06:44,332 --> 00:06:45,163
as always,

195
00:06:45,163 --> 00:06:47,430
live where food can be grown.

196
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:50,124
So if Siberia is this hard to live

197
00:06:50,124 --> 00:06:50,392
in,

198
00:06:50,392 --> 00:06:52,869
why does it belong to Russia at all?

199
00:06:52,869 --> 00:06:53,941
To answer that,

200
00:06:53,941 --> 00:06:56,552
we need to go back five hundred years,

201
00:06:56,552 --> 00:06:58,360
to a much smaller country.

202
00:07:01,310 --> 00:07:03,123
In the thirteenth century,

203
00:07:03,123 --> 00:07:06,078
the Mongols conquered the lands of the Rus.

204
00:07:06,078 --> 00:07:08,093
For around two hundred years,

205
00:07:08,093 --> 00:07:11,250
the Russian princes paid tribute to the Golden

206
00:07:11,250 --> 00:07:11,720
Horde.

207
00:07:12,070 --> 00:07:15,462
Moscow grew powerful as the collector of that

208
00:07:15,462 --> 00:07:16,125
tribute.

209
00:07:16,125 --> 00:07:17,084
And in 1480,

210
00:07:17,084 --> 00:07:18,706
under Ivan the Third,

211
00:07:18,706 --> 00:07:20,770
it stopped paying for good.

212
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:22,127
At that time,

213
00:07:22,127 --> 00:07:25,653
Moscow's state was a fraction of today's Russia.

214
00:07:25,653 --> 00:07:28,243
A land of forests and rivers in the

215
00:07:28,243 --> 00:07:28,675
west,

216
00:07:28,675 --> 00:07:30,330
far from any warm sea.

217
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:31,148
Then,

218
00:07:31,148 --> 00:07:31,850
in 1552,

219
00:07:31,850 --> 00:07:33,175
Ivan the Fourth,

220
00:07:33,175 --> 00:07:35,359
known as Ivan the Terrible,

221
00:07:35,359 --> 00:07:37,308
took Kazan on the Volga.

222
00:07:37,308 --> 00:07:40,193
A few years later he took Astrakhan,

223
00:07:40,193 --> 00:07:43,390
at the river's mouth on the Caspian Sea.

224
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:45,303
Now the whole Volga,

225
00:07:45,303 --> 00:07:47,089
Europe's longest river,

226
00:07:47,089 --> 00:07:48,726
was in Russian hands.

227
00:07:48,726 --> 00:07:51,330
And the road to the east lay open.

228
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:55,075
What pulled the Russians east was not farmland.

229
00:07:55,075 --> 00:07:55,924
It was fur.

230
00:07:55,924 --> 00:07:56,419
Sable,

231
00:07:56,419 --> 00:07:59,178
fox and ermine were worth a fortune in

232
00:07:59,178 --> 00:08:00,380
Europe and Asia,

233
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:03,280
and Siberia's forests were full of them.

234
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:04,549
Around 1581,

235
00:08:04,549 --> 00:08:06,810
a Cossack leader called Yermak,

236
00:08:06,810 --> 00:08:10,132
paid by the wealthy Stroganov merchant family,

237
00:08:10,132 --> 00:08:13,100
crossed the Urals with a few hundred men.

238
00:08:13,450 --> 00:08:15,755
In 1582 they captured Qashliq,

239
00:08:15,755 --> 00:08:18,506
the capital of the Khanate of Sibir,

240
00:08:18,506 --> 00:08:20,960
near the modern city of Tobolsk.

241
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:24,009
The name of that small khanate would one

242
00:08:24,009 --> 00:08:26,091
day cover the whole region:

243
00:08:26,091 --> 00:08:26,760
Siberia.

244
00:08:27,110 --> 00:08:29,288
Yermak died a few years later.

245
00:08:29,288 --> 00:08:32,310
But the door he opened never closed again.

246
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:35,881
What followed was one of the fastest expansions

247
00:08:35,881 --> 00:08:36,686
in history.

248
00:08:36,686 --> 00:08:39,840
Small groups of Cossacks and fur hunters moved

249
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:41,249
from river to river,

250
00:08:41,249 --> 00:08:44,537
carrying their boats over the short gaps between

251
00:08:44,537 --> 00:08:44,940
them.

252
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:46,307
Each time,

253
00:08:46,307 --> 00:08:48,711
they built a wooden fort.

254
00:08:48,711 --> 00:08:50,282
Tobolsk in 1587.

255
00:08:50,282 --> 00:08:51,669
Tomsk in 1604.

256
00:08:51,669 --> 00:08:53,240
Yakutsk in 1632.

257
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:57,216
Many of those forts are still the region's

258
00:08:57,216 --> 00:08:58,510
cities today.

259
00:08:58,860 --> 00:08:59,569
In 1639,

260
00:08:59,569 --> 00:09:03,117
Russian explorers reached the Pacific Ocean,

261
00:09:03,117 --> 00:09:04,930
at the Sea of Okhotsk.

262
00:09:04,930 --> 00:09:08,083
From the Urals to the Pacific had taken

263
00:09:08,083 --> 00:09:10,290
them less than sixty years.

264
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:12,291
The system was simple.

265
00:09:12,291 --> 00:09:15,089
The forts collected a tribute in furs,

266
00:09:15,089 --> 00:09:16,094
called yasak,

267
00:09:16,094 --> 00:09:18,678
from the native peoples of Siberia.

268
00:09:18,678 --> 00:09:20,472
The furs went to Moscow,

269
00:09:20,472 --> 00:09:22,840
and paid for the next push east.

270
00:09:23,190 --> 00:09:24,575
And here's the key.

271
00:09:24,575 --> 00:09:27,762
The Russians didn't need many people to claim

272
00:09:27,762 --> 00:09:28,385
Siberia.

273
00:09:28,385 --> 00:09:29,286
A few forts,

274
00:09:29,286 --> 00:09:30,949
a few hundred men each,

275
00:09:30,949 --> 00:09:33,858
were enough to hold territory the size of

276
00:09:33,858 --> 00:09:34,620
countries.

277
00:09:34,970 --> 00:09:37,289
That's the secret of Russia's map.

278
00:09:37,289 --> 00:09:39,940
It was claimed by a handful of hunters,

279
00:09:39,940 --> 00:09:42,325
not settled by millions of farmers.

280
00:09:42,325 --> 00:09:43,717
The land came first.

281
00:09:43,717 --> 00:09:45,970
The people never really followed.

282
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:47,846
If you're enjoying this,

283
00:09:47,846 --> 00:09:48,517
subscribe.

284
00:09:48,517 --> 00:09:50,897
Every week we find the story hidden in

285
00:09:50,897 --> 00:09:51,690
another map.

286
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:57,841
But fur alone doesn't explain why Russia kept

287
00:09:57,841 --> 00:09:59,442
growing for centuries.

288
00:09:59,442 --> 00:10:00,138
For that,

289
00:10:00,138 --> 00:10:02,365
look at the land around Moscow.

290
00:10:02,365 --> 00:10:03,131
It's flat.

291
00:10:03,131 --> 00:10:04,732
Almost perfectly flat,

292
00:10:04,732 --> 00:10:06,750
for thousands of kilometers.

293
00:10:07,100 --> 00:10:09,720
There are no high mountains and no wide

294
00:10:09,720 --> 00:10:12,472
seas to protect the heart of the country.

295
00:10:12,472 --> 00:10:15,419
Armies can march straight in from almost any

296
00:10:15,419 --> 00:10:16,140
direction.

297
00:10:16,490 --> 00:10:17,862
And they did.

298
00:10:17,862 --> 00:10:21,683
The Mongols in the thirteenth century.

299
00:10:21,683 --> 00:10:25,993
A Polish army that occupied Moscow in 1610.

300
00:10:25,993 --> 00:10:27,561
Sweden in 1708.

301
00:10:27,561 --> 00:10:29,324
Napoleon in 1812.

302
00:10:29,324 --> 00:10:30,990
Germany in 1941.

303
00:10:31,340 --> 00:10:34,654
Napoleon reached Moscow in 1812 with a huge

304
00:10:34,654 --> 00:10:35,106
army.

305
00:10:35,106 --> 00:10:38,043
But Russia was so big that he couldn't

306
00:10:38,043 --> 00:10:39,474
force a surrender.

307
00:10:39,474 --> 00:10:40,755
The city burned,

308
00:10:40,755 --> 00:10:41,734
winter came,

309
00:10:41,734 --> 00:10:44,671
and his army was destroyed on the long

310
00:10:44,671 --> 00:10:45,500
road home.

311
00:10:45,850 --> 00:10:48,991
Russian leaders drew a lesson from this.

312
00:10:48,991 --> 00:10:50,984
If you can't build walls,

313
00:10:50,984 --> 00:10:52,210
build distance.

314
00:10:52,210 --> 00:10:55,581
The more land between the enemy and Moscow,

315
00:10:55,581 --> 00:10:57,190
the safer Moscow is.

316
00:10:57,540 --> 00:11:00,067
Historians call this strategic depth.

317
00:11:00,067 --> 00:11:02,993
And it helps explain why the Russian Empire

318
00:11:02,993 --> 00:11:05,254
kept pushing its borders outward,

319
00:11:05,254 --> 00:11:06,185
towards seas,

320
00:11:06,185 --> 00:11:09,310
mountains and deserts that could act as walls.

321
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:12,398
Peter the Great fought his way to the

322
00:11:12,398 --> 00:11:15,641
Baltic and founded Saint Petersburg in 1703.

323
00:11:15,641 --> 00:11:16,145
Later,

324
00:11:16,145 --> 00:11:18,595
the empire reached the Black Sea,

325
00:11:18,595 --> 00:11:22,198
the Caucasus mountains and the deserts of Central

326
00:11:22,198 --> 00:11:22,630
Asia.

327
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:23,966
In the east,

328
00:11:23,966 --> 00:11:26,848
the empire crossed the sea to Alaska.

329
00:11:26,848 --> 00:11:28,819
In the eighteen hundreds,

330
00:11:28,819 --> 00:11:32,308
Russian America even had trading posts as far

331
00:11:32,308 --> 00:11:33,900
south as California.

332
00:11:34,250 --> 00:11:37,084
But Alaska was even harder to hold than

333
00:11:37,084 --> 00:11:37,721
Siberia.

334
00:11:37,721 --> 00:11:39,422
It was far from Moscow,

335
00:11:39,422 --> 00:11:41,264
very expensive to supply,

336
00:11:41,264 --> 00:11:44,310
and the fur animals were being hunted out.

337
00:11:44,660 --> 00:11:45,544
So in 1867,

338
00:11:45,544 --> 00:11:48,783
Russia sold Alaska to the United States for

339
00:11:48,783 --> 00:11:51,213
seven point two million dollars.

340
00:11:51,213 --> 00:11:53,790
That's roughly two cents per acre.

341
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:56,680
It's one of the few times Russia ever

342
00:11:56,680 --> 00:11:57,749
gave land away.

343
00:11:57,749 --> 00:12:00,155
And it shows the problem perfectly:

344
00:12:00,155 --> 00:12:02,494
claiming a territory is one thing,

345
00:12:02,494 --> 00:12:05,502
but filling it with people is something else

346
00:12:05,502 --> 00:12:06,170
entirely.

347
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:11,667
By the late eighteen hundreds,

348
00:12:11,667 --> 00:12:14,461
the empire had a serious problem.

349
00:12:14,461 --> 00:12:16,269
Siberia was enormous,

350
00:12:16,269 --> 00:12:17,830
rich in resources,

351
00:12:17,830 --> 00:12:20,460
and almost impossible to reach.

352
00:12:20,810 --> 00:12:23,703
Travel from Moscow to the Pacific could take

353
00:12:23,703 --> 00:12:24,218
months,

354
00:12:24,218 --> 00:12:26,983
by river boat in summer and by horse-drawn

355
00:12:26,983 --> 00:12:28,140
sledge in winter.

356
00:12:28,490 --> 00:12:29,391
So in 1891,

357
00:12:29,391 --> 00:12:33,143
Russia began building the Trans-Siberian Railway,

358
00:12:33,143 --> 00:12:36,294
from Moscow all the way to Vladivostok on

359
00:12:36,294 --> 00:12:37,270
the Pacific.

360
00:12:37,620 --> 00:12:40,503
The main line is about nine thousand three

361
00:12:40,503 --> 00:12:42,179
hundred kilometers long.

362
00:12:42,179 --> 00:12:45,062
It's still the longest railway line in the

363
00:12:45,062 --> 00:12:45,531
world,

364
00:12:45,531 --> 00:12:48,280
and the full journey takes about a week.

365
00:12:48,630 --> 00:12:52,219
Crews laid track across swamps and permafrost,

366
00:12:52,219 --> 00:12:55,349
and around Lake Baikal they had to blast

367
00:12:55,349 --> 00:12:57,334
tunnels along the cliffs.

368
00:12:57,334 --> 00:12:58,709
The last section,

369
00:12:58,709 --> 00:13:00,388
along the Amur River,

370
00:13:00,388 --> 00:13:02,450
was only finished in 1916.

371
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:03,608
Lake Baikal,

372
00:13:03,608 --> 00:13:04,354
by the way,

373
00:13:04,354 --> 00:13:06,219
is the deepest lake on Earth,

374
00:13:06,219 --> 00:13:08,830
more than one and a half kilometers deep.

375
00:13:08,830 --> 00:13:11,255
It holds around a fifth of the world's

376
00:13:11,255 --> 00:13:13,120
unfrozen fresh surface water.

377
00:13:13,470 --> 00:13:16,513
The railway did what the forts never could.

378
00:13:16,513 --> 00:13:17,828
It brought people.

379
00:13:17,828 --> 00:13:20,110
Millions of peasants moved east,

380
00:13:20,110 --> 00:13:22,600
and new cities grew along the line.

381
00:13:22,950 --> 00:13:25,476
Novosibirsk was founded in 1893,

382
00:13:25,476 --> 00:13:28,537
where the railway crossed the Ob River.

383
00:13:28,537 --> 00:13:31,599
Today it's Russia's third largest city,

384
00:13:31,599 --> 00:13:34,890
with around one and a half million people.

385
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:37,673
But notice where the railway runs:

386
00:13:37,673 --> 00:13:39,897
along the far south of Siberia,

387
00:13:39,897 --> 00:13:41,983
where the climate is mildest.

388
00:13:41,983 --> 00:13:44,346
So the settlers stayed there too,

389
00:13:44,346 --> 00:13:47,683
which is exactly why Siberia's cities form that

390
00:13:47,683 --> 00:13:49,490
thin southern line today.

391
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:51,566
In the twentieth century,

392
00:13:51,566 --> 00:13:54,355
the Soviet Union pushed into the north as

393
00:13:54,355 --> 00:13:54,753
well.

394
00:13:54,753 --> 00:13:56,214
It wanted the metals,

395
00:13:56,214 --> 00:13:56,878
the coal,

396
00:13:56,878 --> 00:13:59,334
the oil and the gas buried under the

397
00:13:59,334 --> 00:14:00,330
frozen ground.

398
00:14:00,680 --> 00:14:04,034
Many northern towns were first built with forced

399
00:14:04,034 --> 00:14:05,950
labor from the Gulag camps.

400
00:14:05,950 --> 00:14:06,429
Later,

401
00:14:06,429 --> 00:14:09,851
the state offered higher wages and extra holidays

402
00:14:09,851 --> 00:14:12,110
to attract workers to the north.

403
00:14:12,460 --> 00:14:13,057
Norilsk,

404
00:14:13,057 --> 00:14:14,715
above the Arctic Circle,

405
00:14:14,715 --> 00:14:17,833
became one of the northernmost large cities in

406
00:14:17,833 --> 00:14:18,563
the world,

407
00:14:18,563 --> 00:14:21,747
with more than one hundred and seventy thousand

408
00:14:21,747 --> 00:14:22,277
people,

409
00:14:22,277 --> 00:14:24,400
built around huge nickel mines.

410
00:14:24,750 --> 00:14:26,107
For a few decades,

411
00:14:26,107 --> 00:14:29,391
it looked like Siberia might finally fill up.

412
00:14:29,391 --> 00:14:32,175
But that growth depended on one thing:

413
00:14:32,175 --> 00:14:33,960
the state paying for it.

414
00:14:36,910 --> 00:14:37,538
In 1991,

415
00:14:37,538 --> 00:14:39,492
the Soviet Union collapsed.

416
00:14:39,492 --> 00:14:40,539
The subsidies,

417
00:14:40,539 --> 00:14:43,749
the northern bonuses and the cheap flights to

418
00:14:43,749 --> 00:14:46,890
the rest of the country largely disappeared.

419
00:14:47,240 --> 00:14:48,503
And people left.

420
00:14:48,503 --> 00:14:49,245
Chukotka,

421
00:14:49,245 --> 00:14:51,177
the region facing Alaska,

422
00:14:51,177 --> 00:14:54,445
lost around two thirds of its population in

423
00:14:54,445 --> 00:14:56,450
little more than a decade.

424
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:59,388
Across the whole Russian Far East,

425
00:14:59,388 --> 00:15:02,715
the population fell by roughly a fifth after

426
00:15:02,715 --> 00:15:03,158
1991,

427
00:15:03,158 --> 00:15:05,154
as people moved back west,

428
00:15:05,154 --> 00:15:07,890
towards Moscow and the warmer south.

429
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:11,054
The government has tried to reverse it.

430
00:15:11,054 --> 00:15:11,898
Since 2016,

431
00:15:11,898 --> 00:15:14,783
any Russian citizen can apply for a free

432
00:15:14,783 --> 00:15:17,104
hectare of land in the Far East.

433
00:15:17,104 --> 00:15:20,270
Few people have actually moved there for it.

434
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:22,871
Yet Siberia is far from useless.

435
00:15:22,871 --> 00:15:25,804
Much of Russia's oil and natural gas comes

436
00:15:25,804 --> 00:15:28,874
from beneath Western Siberia and the Arctic,

437
00:15:28,874 --> 00:15:31,535
and those exports pay for a large part

438
00:15:31,535 --> 00:15:33,240
of the country's budget.

439
00:15:33,590 --> 00:15:36,425
But modern oil and gas fields don't need

440
00:15:36,425 --> 00:15:37,255
big cities.

441
00:15:37,255 --> 00:15:39,883
Workers often fly in for a few weeks,

442
00:15:39,883 --> 00:15:41,335
then fly home again.

443
00:15:41,335 --> 00:15:42,649
The wealth leaves,

444
00:15:42,649 --> 00:15:44,170
and so do the people.

445
00:15:44,520 --> 00:15:46,715
And now the climate is changing.

446
00:15:46,715 --> 00:15:49,576
Siberia is warming faster than most of the

447
00:15:49,576 --> 00:15:50,108
planet,

448
00:15:50,108 --> 00:15:53,301
and the permafrost under some towns is starting

449
00:15:53,301 --> 00:15:53,900
to thaw.

450
00:15:54,250 --> 00:15:57,489
That could open new farmland and longer shipping

451
00:15:57,489 --> 00:15:59,604
seasons along the Arctic coast.

452
00:15:59,604 --> 00:16:01,521
But it also threatens roads,

453
00:16:01,521 --> 00:16:05,090
pipelines and buildings that were designed for ground

454
00:16:05,090 --> 00:16:06,280
that never melts.

455
00:16:06,630 --> 00:16:08,860
So even with a warmer climate,

456
00:16:08,860 --> 00:16:11,738
Siberia is unlikely to fill up any time

457
00:16:11,738 --> 00:16:12,170
soon.

458
00:16:12,520 --> 00:16:14,730
So let's put the whole story together.

459
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:17,832
Russia is so big because a small state

460
00:16:17,832 --> 00:16:20,797
on an open plain kept pushing outward for

461
00:16:20,797 --> 00:16:21,643
protection,

462
00:16:21,643 --> 00:16:24,749
and because fur made it worth racing across

463
00:16:24,749 --> 00:16:25,384
Siberia,

464
00:16:25,384 --> 00:16:27,360
all the way to the Pacific.

465
00:16:27,710 --> 00:16:30,440
And it's so empty because most of that

466
00:16:30,440 --> 00:16:31,560
land is frozen,

467
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:33,170
far from any warm sea,

468
00:16:33,170 --> 00:16:36,180
crossed by rivers flowing into the Arctic,

469
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:39,050
with summers too short for most farming.

470
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:41,691
The land was claimed by hunters,

471
00:16:41,691 --> 00:16:43,495
connected by one railway,

472
00:16:43,495 --> 00:16:46,411
and settled only along its southern edge.

473
00:16:46,411 --> 00:16:48,216
Four hundred years later,

474
00:16:48,216 --> 00:16:51,548
most Russians still live where the story began:

475
00:16:51,548 --> 00:16:52,450
in the west.

476
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:57,086
Every map hides a story like this one.

477
00:16:57,086 --> 00:17:01,263
If you want to discover the next one,

478
00:17:01,263 --> 00:17:04,450
subscribe to Timeline Earth.
